## Big picture

### Where this chapter fits

Part I ended with [Chapter 6](../part1/ch06-simple-decisions.qmd), which chose a *single* action to maximize expected utility. Most real problems are sequences: each action changes the situation, which changes what the next action should be. Part II (Chapters 7–14) handles such **sequential decisions** when the current state is always known. This chapter is its foundation: it solves them *exactly*, assuming the rules of the world are fully known. Every later chapter relaxes something:
- huge state spaces ([Ch 8](ch08-approximate-value-functions.qmd), [Ch 9](ch09-online-planning.qmd));
- searching over policies directly (Ch 10–13);
- unknown rules that must be learned (Part III);
- a hidden state (Part IV, starting at [Ch 19](../part4/ch19-beliefs.qmd)).

**The question:** if you know exactly how the world responds to your actions (with probabilities) and what each outcome is worth, what should you do in every situation to collect the most reward over time?

The short answer: **compute what every situation is worth when you act well from it, then in each situation take the action that leads to the best mix of immediate reward and future worth.** Policy iteration and value iteration are two ways to compute those worths.

